**Question.** Is it consistent with ZFC that $|\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})| > \aleph_\omega$?

**Answer.** Yes.

**Proof.**

Recall $|\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})| = 2^{\aleph_0}$, the cardinality of the continuum. We show that the value of $2^{\aleph_0}$ can consistently be pushed strictly above $\aleph_\omega$.

**Step 1 (Ground model).** Start from a model $V \models \mathrm{ZFC} + \mathrm{GCH}$. By Gödel's constructibility argument, $\mathrm{ZFC} + \mathrm{GCH}$ is consistent relative to $\mathrm{ZFC}$ (if $\mathrm{ZFC}$ is consistent, so is $\mathrm{ZFC}+\mathrm{GCH}$). In $V$, we have $2^{\aleph_0} = \aleph_1$ and, more generally, $2^{\aleph_n} = \aleph_{n+1}$ for all $n < \omega$, so $\aleph_\omega = \sup_{n<\omega} \aleph_n$ is a singular strong limit cardinal of cofinality $\omega$.

**Step 2 (Forcing notion).** Let $\kappa = \aleph_{\omega+1}$. Note $\kappa$ is a regular cardinal (every successor cardinal is regular). Consider the Cohen forcing $\mathbb{P} = \mathrm{Fn}(\kappa \times \omega, 2)$, the partial order of finite partial functions from $\kappa \times \omega$ to $2$, ordered by reverse inclusion. Equivalently, $\mathbb{P}$ is the forcing to add $\kappa$-many Cohen reals.

**Step 3 (Cardinal/cofinality preservation).** $\mathbb{P}$ has the $\aleph_1$-c.c. (in fact the $\aleph_0$-c.c., since it is a finite-support product indexed by $\kappa$; the c.c.c. is preserved under arbitrary-support products of c.c.c. forcings — the standard $\Delta$-system / c.c.c. product theorem). Because $\mathbb{P}$ has the $\aleph_1$-c.c. and $\kappa = \aleph_{\omega+1}$ is regular with $\operatorname{cf}(\kappa) = \kappa > \omega$, forcing with $\mathbb{P}$ preserves all cardinals and cofinalities. In particular $\aleph_\omega$ and $\aleph_{\omega+1}$ remain cardinals in the extension, with $\aleph_\omega < \aleph_{\omega+1}$.

**Step 4 (The continuum in the extension).** In $V^{\mathbb{P}}$, the generic object yields $\kappa$-many distinct Cohen reals $\{r_\alpha : \alpha < \kappa\}$, so $2^{\aleph_0} \geq \kappa = \aleph_{\omega+1}$ in the extension.

For the upper bound: every real (every element of $2^\omega$) in $V^{\mathbb{P}}$ has a name in $V$. Since $\mathbb{P}$ has the $\aleph_1$-c.c., every name for a real is decided by a countable antichain, and the number of such names is bounded by
$$
|\mathbb{P}|^{\aleph_0} = \kappa^{\aleph_0}.
$$
Under GCH in $V$, with $\kappa = \aleph_{\omega+1}$ regular (hence $\operatorname{cf}(\kappa) > \aleph_0$), the Hausdorff formula / König-type computation gives $\kappa^{\aleph_0} = \kappa$. Concretely, $\aleph_{\omega+1}^{\aleph_0} = \aleph_{\omega+1}$ holds in $V \models \mathrm{GCH}$ because $\aleph_{\omega+1}$ is a successor cardinal with uncountable cofinality and GCH collapses the relevant exponentiation to the cardinal itself. Hence the number of nice names for reals is at most $\kappa$, so
$$
2^{\aleph_0} \leq \kappa = \aleph_{\omega+1} \quad \text{in } V^{\mathbb{P}}.
$$

Combining the two inequalities:
$$
2^{\aleph_0} = \aleph_{\omega+1} > \aleph_\omega \quad \text{in } V^{\mathbb{P}}.
$$

**Step 5 (Consistency).** $V^{\mathbb{P}} \models \mathrm{ZFC}$ (forcing preserves ZFC) and $V^{\mathbb{P}} \models 2^{\aleph_0} = \aleph_{\omega+1} > \aleph_\omega$. Therefore
$$
\mathrm{Con}(\mathrm{ZFC}) \;\Longrightarrow\; \mathrm{Con}\!\bigl(\mathrm{ZFC} + 2^{\aleph_0} > \aleph_\omega\bigr).
$$

**Remark (Easton's theorem, stronger form).** In fact Easton's theorem gives much more: on the class of regular cardinals, the continuum function $\kappa \mapsto 2^\kappa$ can be any class function $F$ that is (i) non-decreasing and (ii) satisfies $\operatorname{cf}(F(\kappa)) > \kappa$. Taking $F(\aleph_0) = \aleph_{\omega+1}$ (regular, $\operatorname{cf} = \aleph_{\omega+1} > \aleph_0$) is admissible, yielding a model of $\mathrm{ZFC}$ in which $2^{\aleph_0} = \aleph_{\omega+1}$. One can even take $F(\aleph_0) = \aleph_{\omega_1+5}$ or any regular cardinal above $\aleph_\omega$, so the continuum can be made arbitrarily far above $\aleph_\omega$.

**Conclusion.** It is consistent with ZFC (assuming ZFC itself is consistent) that $|\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})| = 2^{\aleph_0} = \aleph_{\omega+1} > \aleph_\omega$. Hence yes, it is consistent with ZFC that $|\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})| > \aleph_\omega$.

